---
import { site } from '../data/site';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const description =
  'Software engineer and technical lead in Linz, Austria, working on agent infrastructure, multi-agent orchestration, and developer tooling.';
---

<Base title="About" description={description} current="about">
  <main id="main">
    <div class="in">
      <div class="abouthead">
        <div>
          <h1>About</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            I am a software engineer and technical lead in Linz, Austria. My background is distributed production systems
            and observability. Today I specialize in agent infrastructure, multi-agent orchestration, developer tooling,
            and AI-native software engineering.
          </p>
          <p class="lede">
            I design and operate human-in-the-loop agent systems with runtime guardrails and measurement-driven
            optimization, and I publish open-source developer tooling for AI coding agents.
          </p>
          <a class="cta" href={site.links.linkedin}>Full profile on LinkedIn &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <img
          src="/images/about/richard-ertl.webp"
          alt="Portrait of Richard Ertl, smiling, in a dark blazer over a white T-shirt, against a plain light wall"
          width="800"
          height="1000"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="s" id="how-it-started">
      <div class="in">
        <h2 class="sec">How it started</h2>
        <div class="story">
          <p class="ask">
            If AI agents are writing the code, why are we still working in environments designed for humans to write it?
          </p>
          <p>That's the question that started my journey a few months ago.</p>
          <p>
            I believe developer tools need to evolve as our role shifts from writing code to guiding, evaluating, and
            improving AI-driven development.
          </p>
          <p>My approach follows three steps:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Make it visible.</li>
            <li>Make it measurable and comparable.</li>
            <li>Optimize what matters.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            It all starts with visibility. But how do we know what information matters before we can actually see it?
            That's why I've been building flexible tools rather than fixed dashboards.
          </p>
          <p>
            One of them is open source: <a href="/projects/aihud/">AIHUD</a>, a lightweight, highly customizable
            heads-up display for AI coding agents. Choose what matters to you, customize your layout, or build your own
            visualizations.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="s">
      <div class="in">
        <h2 class="sec">Areas of specialization</h2>
        <div class="rg two">
          <div>
            <h3>Agentic AI and developer tooling</h3>
            <p>Agent infrastructure, multi-agent systems, human-in-the-loop systems, runtime guardrails.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Evaluation and optimization</h3>
            <p>Observability, telemetry, tracing, evaluation gates, token and context optimization.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Architecture and distributed systems</h3>
            <p>Software architecture, control planes, task orchestration, state isolation, idempotent handovers.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Machine learning</h3>
            <p>Classical machine learning and deep learning.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="s path">
      <div class="in">
        <h2 class="sec">Experience</h2>
        <ol class="tl long">
          <li>
            <b>2026</b>
            <div>
              <h3>Independent R&amp;D</h3>
              <small>2026 to present</small>
            </div>
            <p>
              <a href="/projects/systemprinter/">SystemPrinter</a>, a private agentic software engineering system, and{' '}
              <a href="/projects/aihud/">AIHUD</a>, an open-source live display for AI coding agent sessions.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>2023</b>
            <div>
              <h3>Software Engineer, Project and Technical Lead</h3>
              <small>Otelo eGen, March 2023 to September 2026</small>
            </div>
            <p>
              TalentSpace, an interactive technology environment commissioned by the Upper Austrian Chamber of Commerce.
              End-to-end technical responsibility for architecture, core software, hardware integration, delivery, and
              operation of 14 stations with 200+ active services, leading a project team of five.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>2022</b>
            <div>
              <h3>Software Developer</h3>
              <small>BMW Steyr, March 2022 to February 2023</small>
            </div>
            <p>
              Contributed to a new process control system for the plant's conversion to Gen6 electric-drive production,
              with web-based tooling in TypeScript to intercept, inspect, and modify Apache Flink messages on real plant
              data streams.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>2014</b>
            <div>
              <h3>Automation Technician</h3>
              <small>MAHLE Vöcklabruck, April 2014 to July 2019</small>
            </div>
            <p>
              Industrial automation and PLC programming with Siemens SIMATIC technologies on the largest production line.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="s">
      <div class="in">
        <h2 class="sec">Education</h2>
        <div class="rg two">
          <div>
            <h3>MSc Mobile Computing</h3>
            <p>
              Graduated with distinction, 2025. University of Applied Sciences Upper Austria, Hagenberg Campus. Thesis on
              the design, analysis, and optimization of a centralized real-time monitoring system for the distributed
              TalentSpace platform.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>BSc Automotive Computing</h3>
            <p>
              Graduated with distinction, 2022. University of Applied Sciences Upper Austria, Hagenberg Campus. Thesis on
              parking-duration prediction and parking-space allocation using real-world data.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="s research">
      <div class="in">
        <h2 class="sec">Publication</h2>
        <p class="cite">
          Müller, A., Ertl, R., Gutenbrunner, S., Scheuchenpflug, M., and Ostermayer, G. (2026).{' '}
          <a href={site.links.publication}
            >Optimizing Parking Efficiency Using Parking Duration Prediction: A Case Study of a Shopping Mall Parking
            Facility</a
          >. Applied Sciences, 16(13), 6704. Peer-reviewed research derived from my Bachelor's thesis. DOI:
          10.3390/app16136704.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
